combine flexibility and strong light absorption, a property that inspired us to construct 2D MOFs based on oligosilyl linkers. While organosilicon species have been previously used in MOF formation,[17-20] the organic linker usuall contains a single Si atom, which precludes access to properties inherent to o-conjugated extended oligosilanes.
We report the first set of many possibilities for oligosilyl MOFs. The MOFs consist of Cu paddlewheel nodes connected by linear permethylated silicon chains, $\mathsf{S i}_{\mathsf{n}}\mathsf{M e}_{2\mathsf{n}}(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{C O}_{2}\mathsf{H})_{2}$ (lin$\mathsf{S i}_{\mathsf{n}}$ $\mathtt{,n=2,4}$ )into a two-dimensional network. Scheme 1 illustrates the assembly of the $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ ions and linkers into a layered 2D structure which is then transformed upon solvent exchange.We demonstrate structural control over the arrangement of the 2D sheets using simple solvent exchanges and heat treatment. As 2D MOFs are increasingly attractive materials for a range of applications due to their processability and layer control,[21-25] oligosilyl MOFs are well-positioned as a structurally tunable materials for a range of applications from sensing and molecular entrapment to optical and electronic applications.
Under solvothermal conditions, combining copper (ll) salts with lin- $\cdot\mathsf{S i}_{2}$ and lin- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathsf{S i}_{4}$ yielded dark blue single crystals. Single Crystal X-Ray Diffraction revealed the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{\mathsf{n}})$ MOFs share similar structural motifs (Figure 1). Each MOF is composed of water-bound Cu paddlewheel nodes connected in a network of two-dimensional sheets which are staggered in an AB fashion. Pore apertures of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ are roughly square-shaped with a width of $17.9\mathsf{A}$ while $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ apertures are oblong with the widest width being $36.3\mathbb{A}$ ：The crystal morphologies of both MOFs closely mirror the structural motifs observed on the molecular level: ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n-S i}_{2})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n-S i}_{4})$ crystals have rectangular and “eye-shaped" morphologies,respectively. The stacking of MOF sheets appears to be driven by van der Waals interactions, as the only point of contact between 2D layers is at the oligosilyl portions of the linkers which align on top of one another. This arrangement of the methylated silicon chains in the MOFs gives rise to hydrophobic channels in the through-plane direction, especially in $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ . The silyl linkers in both cases are also in virtually perfect anti-conformations,[26] with dihedral angles along Si-Si bonds of $180^{\circ}$ for lin- $\cdot\mathsf{S i}_{2}$ and $179.4^{\circ}$ in lin- $\mathrm{\bullet}\mathrm{\dot{s}}_{\mathrm{i_{4}}}$ These dihedral angles suggest the MOF structures hold the oligosilyl linkers in the conformations most conducive to delocalization of electrons across the entire length of each linker.[11,27,28]
Altering the synthesis conditions yielded blue-green polycrystalline powder precipitates of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{\mathsf{n}})$ .Infrared spectroscopy (lR) confirms the presence of the silicon backbone (Figure 2). A single peak at $1246~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ is observed in ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}{\mathsf{S i}}_{2})$ consistent with the $S i\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ stretch.[29] For $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ , a number of $S i\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ peaks are observed in the range of $1230{-}1260~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . In addition, peaks associated with the carboxylic acid groups of the protonated (unbound) linkers at $1682~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ， $1423~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ，and 1295 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are noticeably absent (Figure S9).[30] The difference in wavenumbers between the symmetric and asymmetric COO stretches falls within the range of $130{-}200\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for both materials, which confirms binding of carboxyl groups to two metal atoms as expected in the paddlewheel geometry.[31] Thus, the coordination environment in $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{\mathsf{n}})$ is retained in the polycrystalline powder form.
The 2D nature of these materials allows them to exhibit dynamic structural conversion between different stacking arrangements. These structural rearrangements can be reversibly tuned by solvent exchange, leading to multiple switchable crystalline states. Figure 3a shows the calculated Powder X-Ray Diffraction (PXRD) pattern for the ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}{\mathsf{S i}}_{2})$ crystals, which were formedinahighlypolarsolventmixtureof1:3 ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}I$ diethylformamide (DEF).Exposure of the MOF to a less polar solvent by washing with neat dimethylformamide (DMF),Nmethyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP), or a mixture of dichloromethane (DCM) and acetone yielded crystalline materials with different diffraction patterns. The new PXRD patterns shift progressively to lower 20, with the most drastic structural changes being seen
