maximize contact, while the larger coordinating ligands can displace the bound water and increase the interlayer spacing. Both of these effects contribute to deviations from the symmetric AB stacking and can cause the sheets to shift to minimize steric hindrances. To illustrate this point, we artificially introduced a coordinated acetone molecule to scale in the crystal structure of ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}{\mathsf{S i}}_{2})$ to examine the DCM/acetone solvent system. If all atomic positions in the structure are retained, there is only a ${\sim}1\mathsf{A}$ separation between pendant carbon atoms of the two bound acetone molecules (Figure 5, right). Including the C-H bond lengths would further reduce the separation to well below the length of an average C-C bond. Steric repulsion would therefore necessitate a structural change to alleviate this stress. Simultaneously, the less polar DCM in the solvent system would help accommodate such a change by providing favorable interactions with the permethylated silicon chains. Extending our hypothesis to the most nonpolar solvent system in the pentaneexchanged MOFs, the purely nonpolar solvent environment enables the layers to rearrange with no single preferred orientation, resulting in an amorphous material.
$\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{\mathsf{n}})$ are able to undergo further structural transformations under thermal activation. Thermogravimetric analysis demonstrates the thermal stability of ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n-S i}_{2})$ in an argon atmosphere (Figure 6a).A mass loss event is observed at $205^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ，where $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n-S i}_{2})$ undergoes loss of solvent (primarily DMF). lmmediately following solvent removal, an exothermic peak in the heat flow curve is observed at $224^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in a region where no mass is lost, which suggests desolvation triggers a physical phase change in the framework. Similar thermal behavior was observedfor $C u(l i n{-}S i_{4})$ (Figure 6b); however, taking a closer look at the heat flow (Figure S12) reveals slight differences in behavior between ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{I i n-S i}_{2})$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ . The $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ event peaks at $224^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ whilethe $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ event peaks at $214^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ The shoulder feature and broadness of the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ peak suggest a slower, multi-step process is occurring in contrast to the sudden, sharp transition in $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ . The more rapid transition in $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ is also evidenced by the early onset of the $\mathtt{C u}$ (lin$\mathsf{S i}_{4}^{\cdot\dagger}$ heatflowpeakat $207^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ just before the end of the mass loss which spans from $185-214^{\circ}C$ .Thus, the transition begins upon partial desolvation of the material. We expect that these differences arise from the larger aperture size in ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n-S i}_{4})$ which allows for faster release of guest molecules and prompt rearrangement. Integration of the heat flow peaks also determines the amount of energy released during the transitions. $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n-S i}_{4})$ releases $481.9J/9$ while ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{l i n}{\mathsf{-S i}}_{2})$ releases slightly less at 464 J/g. We postulate that the phase transition of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ is more energetically facile due to the greater flexibility of the four-silicon chain,which allows the MOF to transition more quickly as well as reach a more stable configuration. The longer chain length could also provide $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{4})$ more opportunities to engage in more stabilizing hydrophobic interactions, hence the more stable final state.
To further explore this thermal behavior, $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ is desolvated via solvent exchange with DCM followed by heating under vacuum at $140^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 30 minutes to yield a green clay-like material. In the IR spectra of desolvated $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ in Figure S13, a new asymmetric COO peak appears at $1572~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ . A similar phenomenon has been observed upon heating the analogous CuBDC (and related paddlewheel MOFs) where the IR feature is attributed to a new asymmetric COO stretch.[34-36] In the case of CuBDC, the open axial metal sites associate with oxygen atoms in carboxyl groups of neighboring paddlewheels. The IR spectral changes for desolvated $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ are consistent with reported results in CuBDC and suggest the presence of a similar bridging carboxyl group between adjacent Cu paddlewheels.[36]  The thermally-activated $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{l i n}{-}\mathsf{S i}_{2})$ is largely amorphous by PXRD,
